he target is 58\n For batch 3, when context is [42, 61, 39, 56, 42, 6, 1, 39, 52, 42, 1, 58] -> the target is 46\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n For batch 4, when context is [46] -> the target is 63\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63] -> the target is 6\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6] -> the target is 0\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0] -> the target is 37\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37] -> the target is 53\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53] -> the target is 59\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59] -> the target is 1\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59, 1] -> the target is 45\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59, 1, 45] -> the target is 56\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59, 1, 45, 56] -> the target is 39\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59, 1, 45, 56, 39] -> the target is 60\n For batch 4, when context is [46, 63, 6, 0, 37, 53, 59, 1, 45, 56, 39, 60] -> the target is 43\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":20},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### What is a N-Gram Model?\n\nThe N-gram model was not invented for modern AI, nor does it come from Neural Networks.\n* **The Root**: It is based on Markov Chains, a mathematical concept introduced by Russian mathematician Andrey Markov in 1913. To prove his mathematical theory, Markov manually counted the frequencies of vowels and consonants in Aleksandr Pushkin’s poem Eugene Onegin.\n* **The Application**: In 1948, Claude Shannon (the father of Information Theory) used this exact method to approximate English text. He showed that by simply tracking which letters or words follow each other, a machine could generate text that looks surprisingly human.\n\n\nAn N-gram is simply a sequence of \\(N\\) consecutive items (words or characters) from a given slice of text.The \"\\(N\\)\" stands for the number of items in the window. An N-gram model is a classical, statistical/probabilistic model. It does not use neural networks, it does not use weights, and it does not use backpropagation. It is just a massive look-up table of counts\n\nHere is how a single sentence—\n\"I love learning AI\"—is broken down into different N-grams:\n* Unigram (\\(N=1\\)): Looks at words completely in isolation.[\"I\"], [\"love\"], [\"learning\"], [\"AI\"]\n* Bigram (\\(N=2\\)): Looks at pairs of words.[\"I\", \"love\"], [\"love\", \"learning\"], [\"learning\", \"AI\"]\n* Trigram (\\(N=3\\)): Looks at triplets of words.[\"I\", \"love\", \"learning\"], [\"love\", \"learning\", \"AI\"]","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"A Bigram Language Model is a type of statistical language model that predicts the next word (or character) in a sequence based only on the single previous word (or character).It is the simplest form of an N-gram model (where N=2). \n\nWe shall first try with a bigram model and then advance as needed","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n###  Ultimate Guide to Modern LLMs: From Next-Word Prediction to PyTorch Code\n\nThis document provides a complete conceptual and technical breakdown of how Large Language Models (LLMs) like ChatGPT work under the hood, seamlessly mapping high-level conversational theory to low-level PyTorch tensor operations.\n\n---\n\n#### 🧠 Core Concept: The ChatGPT Architecture Mindmap\n\nWhile modern AI feels like talking to a human, under the hood, **ChatGPT predicts exactly one word at a time**, repeating this loop thousands of times to generate text via **autoregressive generation**.\n\n##### 🔄 1. The Autoregressive Generation Loop\n* **Prediction**: The model reads your input text (prompt) and guesses the single best next word.\n* **Append**: It glues that guessed word directly onto the end of the original prompt.\n* **Feedforward**: It inputs the newly lengthened text back into itself to guess the next word.\n* **Termination**: This loop repeats until the model generates a special hidden token called `[EOS]` (End of Sequence).\n\n###### 🎬 Concrete Step-by-Step Example\nIf you type: `\"The capital of France is\"`\n1. **Step 1**: Reads `\"The capital of France is\"` -> Predicts **`\"Paris\"`**.\n2. **Step 2**: Reads `\"The capital of France is Paris\"` -> Predicts **`\".\"`**.\n3. **Step 3**: Reads `\"The capital of France is Paris.\"` -> Predicts **`\"[EOS]\"`** (Stop).\n\n##### 🗺️ 2. Shifting from Machine Translation to Conversation\n* **Traditional Approach (Seq2Seq)**: Historically, models translated sequences from one distinct lane to another (e.g., English text to French text).\n* **Modern LLM Approach**: The Transformer treats both your question and its answer as **one single, continuous block of text**. \n* **Unified Interface**: It solves coding, translation, and chat identically—by simply completing an unfinished block of text.\n\n##### 💡 3. How Simple Word Prediction Creates Deep Intelligence\n* **Contextual Understanding**: To guess the next word correctly, the model must understand syntax, complex reasoning, and historical facts.\n* **Self-Attention**: This mechanism allows a word generated thousands of tokens later to dynamically look back and depend on everything written earlier.\n* **Emergence**: By training on trillions of next-word patterns across the internet, the model accidentally develops an internal model of how the real world operates.\n\n---\n\n#### 🛠️ Code Architecture: The Neural Bigram Model\n\nTo understand the core tensor shapes used by large Transformers, we look at a **Bigram Model**. It predicts the next token based *only* on the current single token using a lookup grid.\n\n---\n\n#### 🏎️ Deep Dive: PyTorch Execution & Plumbing\n\n##### Why write `model(xb, yb)` instead of `model.forward(xb, yb)`?\nIn PyTorch, you should **never call `.forward()` directly**. \n\nCalling `model(xb, yb)` triggers PyTorch’s internal `__call__` method. Think of it like an amusement park ride entrance:\n* **The Call Gate (`model()`)**: The operator checks your ticket, sets up internal hooks (debugging scripts), tracks historical data for backpropagation gradients, and buckles your seatbelt.\n* **The Manual Fence (`.forward()`)**: Bypasses the gate and jumps straight onto the ride. While it runs, your model misses critical operations required for monitoring and training.\n\n---\n\n#### 📐 Demystifying Tensor Shapes: B, T, and C\n\nLet’s use a physical analogy. Imagine you are a teacher grading **4 separate students** reading at the exact same time. Each student is staring at a sentence containing exactly **3 words**. Your entire vocabulary dictionary holds **10 total unique words**.\n\n| Dimension Symbol | Definition | Classroom Analogy Example | Value |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: |\n| **`B`** | **Batch Size** | The number of independent student data streams processed at once. | `4` |\n| **`T`** | **Time / Block Size** | The length of the sequence (number of words per sentence). | `3` |\n| **`C`** | **Channels / Vocab Size** | The number of output scores generated for every single word. | `10` |\n\n##### Visualizing the 3D Tensor `(B, T, C)`\nWhen your text numbers go into the `token_embedding_table`, they expand into a 3D block of shape **`(4, 3, 10)`**:\n* You hold **4 separate student worksheets** (`B`).\n* Each worksheet contains **3 chronological words** (`T`).\n* Beneath each word sits a list of **10 raw numbers/logits** (`C`), which act as raw popularity votes for which of the 10 dictionary words should appear next.\n\n---\n\n#### 💥 The Flattening Math: Why do we multiply `B * T`?\n\nThe mathematical loss function `f.cross_entropy` is an uncompromising grader. It does not care which student made a guess, nor does it care about temporal sequence order. It treats every single word prediction as an independent multiple-choice question.\n\n##### The Problem\n* Your **logits** are arranged in a 3D structure: `(4, 3, 10)` (Students, Words, Votes).\n* Your **targets** (answers) are arranged in a 2D structure: `(4, 3)` (Students, Words).\n* `f.cross_entropy` refuses to accept these dimensional structures. It demands a flat 2D array of `(Total Questions, Options)` and a flat 1D list of `(Total Answers)`.\n\n##### The Calculation\nWe multiply batch size by time to find the flat volume of independent questions:\n\\[\\text{Total Questions} = B \\times T = 4 \\times 3 = 12 \\text{ independent token predictions}\\]\n\n##### The Solution: `.view()` Transformations\n\n###### 1. Flattening Logits (Guesses)\n```python\nlogits = logits.view(B * T, C)\n```\n* **Before**: A 3D block of `(4, 3, 10)`.\n* **After**: A flat 2D grid of **`(12, 10)`**. It is now a vertical stack of 12 distinct rows, where each row contains 10 voting values.\n\n###### 2. Flattening Targets (Answers)\n```python\ntargets = target.view(B * T)\n```\n* **Before**: A 2D grid of `(4, 3)`.\n* **After**: A single, flat 1D list of **`(12,)`**. It acts as a continuous answer key sheet holding 12 correct integers.\n\n```text\n       3D LOGITS (4, 3, 10)                        FLATTENED 2D LOGITS (12, 10)\n      [ [ [...10 votes...] ] ]                     [ ...10 votes for Word 1... ]\n         [ [...10 votes...] ]                      [ ...10 votes for Word 2... ]\n            [ [...10 votes...] ]                   [ ...10 votes for Word 3... ]\n                     \\                                         ...\n                      \\==== .view(B*T, C) ====>    [ ...10 votes for Word 12... ]\n```\n\nWith dimensions aligned to a 2D matrix of predictions `(12, 10)` and a 1D vector of answers `(12,)`, `f.cross_entropy` compares them row-by-row and produces your final scalar training loss value.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"* **Encoder/Decoder** convert text to numbers, but they do not know what the numbers mean.\n* **`nn.Embedding(vocab_size, vocab_size)`** is a lookup table mapping current tokens (rows) to next tokens (columns).\n* **Logits** are the raw, unadjusted popularity votes stored inside this grid for every possible next token.\n* **Forward Pass** takes your input integer and pulls out its specific row of votes from the table.\n* **`.view()`** flattens the 3D data grid into a 2D matrix so the loss function can read it.\n* **`F.cross_entropy`** compares the model's raw votes against the actual correct target numbers.\n* **Loss** is the final penalty score used by PyTorch to tweak the table's numbers until it guesses right.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.nn import functional as f\n\ntorch.manual_seed(2506)\n\nclass BigramModel(nn.Module):\n\n    def __init__(self, vocab_size):\n        super().__init__()# we are calling the parent class constructor from nn.Module as the constructor in our custom child class of BigramModel\n        self.token_embedding_table = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, vocab_size) \n        #creates an embedding table that correlates every tokens against other to give a probabilistic score or logit for the next predicted word\n\n    def forward(self, idx, target=None):\n        # idx and targets are both (b,t) tensor of integers - [tensor]bxt\n        logits=self.token_embedding_table(idx)\n        #loss=f.cross_entropy(logits, target) # But cross_entropy expects formatted batches, tensors, channels - therefore we need to format our logits and targets\n        \n        if targets is None:\n            loss=None\n        else:\n            B, T, C = logits.shape\n            logits=logits.view(B*T, C)\n            targets=target.view(B*T)\n            loss=f.cross_entropy(logits, targets)\n        return logits, loss\n\n    def generate(self, idx, max_new_tokens):\n        #The transformer is also responsible for generating the very next words in a certain (batch and time ) from the logits and so on\n        for _ in range(max_new_tokesn): # the generation should go on upto a certain token range\n            logits, loss=self(idx) # we first compute the logits from the Embeddings lookup table\n            logits=logits[:,-1] # we are just interested in generating the last tokens or predictions for the next word \n            prob_score=f.softmax(logits, dim=-1) # we calculate probabilistic scores using softmax for the last layer\n            # sample from distribution\n            idx_next=torch.multinomial(prob_scores, num_samples=1)\n            idx=torch.cat((idx, idx_next), dim =1) # concatenate each newly predicted word to the main context\n        return idx\n\nvocab_size=len(vocubulary) # creating object of the class\nmodel=BigramModel(vocab_size)\nlogit_output, loss=model(xb, yb) # we don't call the .forward function hee...as pyTorch does this internally \nprint(loss, logit_output.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-27T05:52:10.059645Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-27T05:52:10.060136Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-27T05:52:10.106784Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-27T05:52:10.060089Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-27T05:52:10.105095Z"}},"outputs":[{"traceback":["\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m","\u001b[0;31mUnboundLocalError\u001b[0m                         Traceback (most recent call last)","\u001b[0;32m/tmp/ipykernel_58/910016113.py\u001b[0m in \u001b[0;36m<cell line: 0>\u001b[0;34m()\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     39\u001b[0m \u001b[0mvocab_size\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m\u001b[0mlen\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m(\u001b[0m\u001b[0mvocubulary\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m)\u001b[0m \u001b[0;31m# creating object of the class\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     40\u0